"The European Rights Retention Community of Practice (CoP) brings together experts and practitioners across Europe to exchange experiences, learn from one another, and advance rights retention strategies.

For our 11th meeting, we are pleased to welcome institutional case studies from Northern and Southern Europe. Aysa Ekanger and Bror-Magnus S. Strand from UiT The Arctic University of Norway—one of Northern Europe's early adopters—and Ignasi Labastida from the University of Barcelona, a recent leader in Southern Europe, will present their institutional processes and progress in adopting rights retention policies. Following the presentations, we will share key updates on the Rights Retention in Practice task force's work. We then invite community members to share their own experiences, highlight ongoing challenges, and collaborate on practical solutions. &nbsp;

If you work at an academic institution, are a funder, or an open science policymaker, please join us to contribute to the conversation."

"The term “preprint” is widely used in scholarly communication, but it does not have an agreed-upon definition across disciplines, platforms, or professional roles.&nbsp;

The National Information Standards Organization (NISO) broadly defines a preprint as a scholarly article made publicly available before it has been peer reviewed, accepted for publication, or formally endorsed. Yet NISO also recognizes that some preprints are never submitted to a traditional publication venue, while others undergo community or other forms of review before being posted.[1]

New forms of feedback complicate this picture further. For example, bioRxiv and medRxiv have endorsed two AI peer-review services, Q.E.D. and Reviewer3, that allow authors to receive AI feedback either before or after posting their preprints.[2]"

"The Open Journals Collective will host a webinar on Thursday, October 15 at 11 am EST / 10:00 am CST. You can register to attend at: https://forms.gle/GcehZuF8tKJ1UCXe9.&nbsp; The webinar will be recorded for those unable to attend the live session.

The webinar is intended primarily for CRL (Center for Research Libraries) members and NERL Core members, but anyone interested in joining OJC is welcome.&nbsp; OJC membership is available via CRL, see: https://www.crl.edu/open-journals-collective-ojc-partnership&nbsp;

The webinar will introduce the Open Journals Collective, a global movement that allows libraries to support diamond open access journals at scale via our library membership program.&nbsp; OJC works with a range of nonprofit publishers to provide sustainable funding and longevity for their journals.&nbsp; And we offer libraries an alternative to the world of commercial big deals and Transformative Agreements.&nbsp; This session will explore our motivations for founding OJC, our vision, operating model and aspirations for the future.&nbsp; Join us to hear more about building a sustainable future for journal publishing!"

"Over the past decade, a range of open access monograph models have emerged, each seeking to expand access while addressing the unique challenges of scholarly book publishing. 

Three years ago, JSTOR partnered with libraries, publishers, and the academic community to launch Path to Open, exploring whether a shared investment model could broaden access to diverse scholarly books while supporting a sustainable future for academic publishing. Drawing on community feedback and early evidence from the first 100 titles after they became open access, the From Pilot to Impact: What Happens When Path to Open Titles Become Open? Insight Brief explores how this community-led program is reaching broader and more diverse audiences, increasing engagement, and strengthening bibliodiversity. &nbsp;

In this webinar, we'll build on those findings with the latest results, showing how the program's impact has continued to grow since the brief was released. Partners from JSTOR, Boston Library Consortium, University of Oregon, Liverpool University Press, and Syracuse University Press will discuss what these insights mean for the future of open access monographs."
